---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Continue from '../components/Continue.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The road ahead"
  description="Where digital product passports are going: machines as readers, materials as subjects, products that report themselves, verification that outlives companies, and borders that recognize one another."
  eyebrow="03 · The road ahead"
>
  <h1>The road ahead</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The passport of the next decade will be read by machines more often than by people,
    will attach to raw materials as readily as to finished goods, and will be expected to
    answer for a product long after its maker has ceased to exist. Each demand below is
    stated together with the mechanism the framework already maintains for it, and the
    section at the end names what genuinely remains ahead.
  </p>

  <section class="plate" aria-labelledby="machines-h">
    <h2 id="machines-h">Machines become the primary readers</h2>
    <p>
      A passport that only a person can read scales to a household; a passport that a
      customs system, an ERP deployment and an auditor's script can read scales to world
      trade. The framework treats machine-readability as the primary obligation: every
      exported record carries stable identifiers, the canonical form is specified to the
      byte, and the same bytes verify identically in independent implementations. A
      statement that exists only as prose in a PDF is not a passport datum, and the
      framework declines to model it as one.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="plate" aria-labelledby="materials-h">
    <h2 id="materials-h">Materials carry passports of their own</h2>
    <p>
      The next questions regulators ask are questions of matter, not of gadgets: where a
      tonne of steel came from, how much recycled content entered a batch, and which
      derived goods carry which obligations. The transformation algebra answers by
      letting a passported quantity split and combine with its quantities balancing at
      every step, so one passported tonne of iron ore becomes passported trade units,
      and a portion of a unit can leave for a screw run without the record losing an
      gram. Obligations travel with the material, in proportion to what each derived
      product actually took.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="plate" aria-labelledby="reporting-h">
    <h2 id="reporting-h">Products begin to report themselves</h2>
    <p>
      Batteries count their cycles; vehicles know their firmware. The framework gives
      machines principals of their own, with keys bound at manufacture and a discipline
      of commit now and reveal later, so a device can withhold what its operator chooses
      but cannot retroactively contradict what it once committed. Products without
      electronics are served too: the capability classes run from silent objects, which
      need no device participation at all, to fully instrumented ones.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="plate" aria-labelledby="outliving-h">
    <h2 id="outliving-h">Verification must outlive the issuer</h2>
    <p>
      A compliance record that disappears with its operator fails the audit that matters
      most. The framework's third tier is the notarized archive: a passport state,
      signed and timestamped, re-served byte-identically decades later with metadata an
      auditor can read. The durability of the record is treated as an operational
      routine, drilled and verified, rather than as an aspiration.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="plate" aria-labelledby="borders-h">
    <h2 id="borders-h">Borders learn to recognize one another</h2>
    <p>
      The certificate a border accepts tomorrow may carry the signature of another
      jurisdiction's authority, and the signature suite of another cryptography era.
      The framework maintains a recognition ladder from document acceptance to full-core
      interop, states willingness explicitly so that a scheme that declines
      interoperability is never brokered around, and carries sovereign signature suites
      beside classical and post-quantum ones on the same carrier. A relay exists for
      pairs divided by protocols, and it is deliberately stateless and names no master,
      because a pair that speaks directly has no need of it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="plate" aria-labelledby="consumer-h">
    <h2 id="consumer-h">Consumers become routine readers</h2>
    <p>
      The scan at the point of sale is the passport's most public moment. The consumer
      presentation renders the record as a readable page, provides a text form suitable
      for screen readers, and releases only the data points a scan policy states, so
      the most accessible surface is also the most disciplined one.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="plate" aria-labelledby="remaining-h">
    <h2 id="remaining-h">What genuinely remains ahead</h2>
    <p>
      Three things are named so that they are not implied. Guided tooling for defining
      new classes of passport is designed-for as registered data, but the class designer
      itself is work the ecosystem has yet to build; authoring a class today is expert
      work against a published schema. Operation of the transparency anchor as a
      genuine multi-party quorum, beyond the reference operator, awaits the federation's
      growth. And the widest gap is not technical at all: the number of products that
      carry passports at all remains small, which is why the framework ships as a
      starter kit that a national registry can adopt rather than as a system that asks
      the world to converge first.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Continue href="/framework/" num="04" title="How it works" desc="The framework in full: identity, events, tiers, and trust." />
</BaseLayout>
